<div class="calc-content-prose">
  <h2>How to Plan Rebar Grids for Concrete Slabs</h2>
  <p>
    Steel reinforcement controls temperature shrinkage cracking and carries tensile loads in concrete. This calculator determines the total linear footage, bar counts, and estimated steel weight for a bidirectional grid:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Enter Slab Dimensions:</strong> Input the length and width of the pour in feet and inches (or meters).</li>
    <li><strong>Select Bar Spacing:</strong> Standard residential slabs use 12-inch or 16-inch on-center (OC) grid spacing. Heavy vehicular pads or commercial foundations may specify 8-inch to 12-inch spacing.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose Rebar Size:</strong> #4 rebar (1/2-inch diameter) is the standard for residential flatwork, while #3 is common for light patios and #5 for heavy structural foundations.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Rebar Requirements by Slab Size (Standard 12″ On-Center Grid, #4 Bar)</h2>
  <p>
    The reference table below displays grid lines, total linear footage (including 10% waste for lap splices), standard 20-foot stock bar quantities, and estimated steel weight:
  </p>

  <div class="table-container table-scroll-wrapper">
