  <h2>Rebar Sizing: #3 vs. #4 vs. #5 Comparison</h2>
  <p>
    US standard rebar sizes correspond to the bar diameter in eighths of an inch:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>#3 Rebar (3/8-inch, 0.376 lb/ft):</strong> Used primarily for residential sidewalks, decorative garden paths, swimming pool shells (shotcrete), and light masonry joint reinforcement.</li>
    <li><strong>#4 Rebar (1/2-inch, 0.668 lb/ft):</strong> The universal standard for residential flatwork including patios, driveways, garage slabs, foundation footings, and basement walls.</li>
    <li><strong>#5 Rebar (5/8-inch, 1.043 lb/ft):</strong> Specified for commercial slabs, retaining walls, suspended structural decks, and footings carrying multistory load-bearing columns.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Lap Splice Rules & Overlap Requirements</h2>
  <p>
    Because steel rebar is sold in fixed stock lengths (typically 20 feet), multiple bars must overlap to transfer tensile forces across joints:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Standard Overlap Rule:</strong> The American Concrete Institute (ACI 318) recommends a minimum contact lap splice of <strong>30 to 40 bar diameters</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>#4 Rebar Lap Distance:</strong> For #4 rebar (0.5-inch diameter), the minimum lap distance is <code>30 × 0.5 in = 15 inches</code> (or 20 inches for critical structural tension splices).</li>
    <li><strong>Tie Wire:</strong> Tie overlaps with 16-gauge black annealed wire using a figure-eight or snap tie to prevent separation during the concrete pour.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Clearance & Rebar Chairs (Critical Placement Rule)</h2>
  <p>
    Rebar must be positioned in the middle to upper third of the slab depth to provide tensile resistance against top loads and temperature shrinkage. Rebar laying flat on the ground provides zero structural reinforcement and is prone to rapid moisture corrosion. Use plastic rebar chairs, concrete "dobies" (brick bolsters), or metal wire stands spaced every 3 to 4 feet to hold the steel grid securely 1.5 to 2 inches off the subgrade.
  </p>
